{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"flinty-unwhite-wolf","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Why\n\nThe plan-review claim gate invalidates a signed attestation whenever the criteria-registry\nstamp (`regver:`) differs from the current one. `regver` hashes the FULL effective routing\nindex — including every criterion's free-text `check` string — so any edit to\n`criteria_routing.json`, however cosmetic, rotates the stamp and invalidates EVERY\noutstanding plan-review attestation repo-wide at once.\n\nMeasured on this repo: renaming one phrase in a single `check` string rotates the stamp\n(`b058e30f0de6a907` -> `23913c1f6987711b`) and would invalidate the attestations held by\n632 tickets (~40 open/in_progress), forcing a full LLM re-review before any of them can be\nclaimed. That is a false invalidation: the attestation truthfully certifies that the plan\npassed the criteria AS THEY STOOD AT SIGNING TIME, and a later wording change does not\nfalsify that statement.\n\nADR 0015 introduced the check to stop a project activating/editing/disabling a criterion and\nthen claiming against a review that never saw it. The concern is real but the remedy is too\nblunt: it cannot distinguish a criterion being tightened from a typo fix, so it charges a\nfull re-review for both. The result is that criteria maintenance is effectively frozen.\n\n## What\n\nGrandfather plan-review attestations across criteria-registry drift: a `regver` mismatch (or\na missing `regver:` line on an older manifest) no longer blocks the claim. The drift is still\nDETECTED and REPORTED — it becomes advisory metadata on the validity result instead of a\nblocking verdict — so `review-plan --status` and the gate UX can still say \"reviewed under an\nolder criteria registry\".\n\nExplicitly unchanged: `stale-code`, `stale-head`, `stale-material`, `stale-reopened` and the\nsignature check all keep blocking. Only the registry-version dimension is grandfathered.\n\nAlso unchanged: the drift-refresh eligibility path (`registry_unchanged` in\n`attest._signature_branch_decision` / `_sidecar_branch_decision` / `drift_floor`). That flag\ndecides whether a cheap refresh may REUSE a prior verdict versus running a full review; it\nnever blocks a claim, and keeping it conservative is correct.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py` — the plan-review branch of `compute_validity`\n  (the `stale-regver` early return): stop returning `valid: False`; annotate the result.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest_gate.py` — the docstring listing possible verdicts.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py` — `registry_version` docstring, which currently\n  states a rotation invalidates a prior attestation.\n- `docs/adr/0053-grandfather-criteria-registry-drift.md` — new ADR, amends ADR 0015.\n- `docs/adr/0015-project-supplied-criteria.md` — add an amended-by pointer.\n- `docs/plan-review-gate.md` — the two verdict lists that name `stale-regver`.\n- Tests listed under Testing.\n\nOUT OF SCOPE: the Success-Criteria -> Acceptance-Criteria rename itself (separate ticket);\nany config surface for forcing re-review on a material criteria change (recorded as future\nwork in the ADR).\n\n## Approach\n\nIn `compute_validity`'s `kind == _MANIFEST_PREFIX` branch, replace the early\n`return _result(False, ..., \"stale-regver\")` with a non-blocking capture: compare the signed\n`regver` against `registry_version(repo_root)` and, on mismatch or absence, record\n`registry_drift = {\"signed\": ..., \"current\": ...}`. Let control fall through to the remaining\nfreshness checks (`stale-code` / `stale-head` / `stale-material`) unchanged. Attach\n`registry_drift` to the returned dict via the existing `_result` helper so every downstream\nresult carries it uniformly.\n\nThe signed `regver` continues to be read from the AUTHENTICATED manifest (`auth_manifest`),\nnever its plaintext mirror — the reporting path must not become a channel for unauthenticated\ndata (ADR 0049 / the opcert binding tests).\n\n## Testing\n\n- `tests/unit/test_compute_validity.py` — two cases currently asserting\n  `verdict == \"stale-regver\"` invert to: valid, with `registry_drift` populated.\n- `tests/unit/test_attestation_invalidation.py` — same inversion for the overlay-change cases.\n- `tests/unit/test_opcert_local_verify_binding.py` — the \"no weakening\" regver case becomes\n  \"grandfathered but reported\"; the PLAINTEXT-corruption case must still prove the signed\n  manifest is authoritative (unchanged assertion for `stale-code`).\n- `tests/unit/test_plan_review_close_gate_heldout.py` — drop `stale-regver` from the\n  verdict->message mapping.\n- NEW: a regression test proving a registry rotation alone leaves a claim UNBLOCKED while\n  `stale-code` on the same attestation still blocks — pinning that only the registry\n  dimension was grandfathered.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A plan-review attestation whose signed `regver` differs from the current\n      `registry_version(repo_root)` returns `valid: True` from `compute_validity`, and the\n      ticket can be claimed without a fresh `review-plan`.\n- [ ] The same result carries `registry_drift` with the signed and current stamps, so the\n      drift is reportable rather than silent.\n- [ ] A manifest with NO `regver:` line is likewise grandfathered (valid), not rejected.\n- [ ] `stale-code`, `stale-head`, `stale-material` and `stale-reopened` still return\n      `valid: False` — proven by a test that rotates the registry AND drifts a dependency\n      file on one attestation and asserts the verdict is `stale-code`.\n- [ ] The signed `regver` is read from the authenticated manifest only; corrupting the\n      plaintext mirror does not change the reported `registry_drift`.\n- [ ] `docs/adr/0053-grandfather-criteria-registry-drift.md` exists, states the amendment to\n      ADR 0015, and records the accepted consequence that a criteria TIGHTENING no longer\n      retroactively forces re-review.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and the full `pytest` suite pass in the worktree.","id":"1f32-f86e-8a51-4d8e","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["gate","plan-review","attestation"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Grandfather plan-review attestations across criteria-registry (regver) drift"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1784935720608007001,"uuid":"aa974638-1ebd-4226-b04d-24a30fd2d0e0"}